= Foundational triad =

BITwiki uses '''Ontology, Epistemology, and Axiology''' as three distinct but interdependent root axes for reasoning about a knowledge system:

* '''Ontology''' — what is; what kinds of entities, processes, distinctions, and relationships are recognized.
* '''Epistemology''' — how we know; how claims are acquired, interpreted, justified, tested, qualified, and revised.
* '''Axiology''' — what matters; what is valued, prioritized, protected, optimized, or pursued.

These are not three metadata fields. They are recurring questions that can be asked of an article, an ontology, an agent, a workflow, a governance decision, or the system itself.

== Why this triad ==

Any knowledge architecture eventually faces three irreducible problems:

# '''What is the system talking about?'''
# '''Why should anyone believe or trust what it says?'''
# '''Why is this knowledge being selected, preserved, or acted upon?'''

Ontology without epistemology can produce an elegant map with no account of why its claims should be trusted. Epistemology without ontology can evaluate claims without being clear about what kinds of things those claims concern. Both without axiology can become directionless: technically coherent but unable to explain why one inquiry, risk, value, or outcome should receive attention over another.

== Ontology — being, structure, relation ==

Ontology asks '''what is'''. In BITwiki this includes practical as well as philosophical questions:

* What kind of entity is this page about?
* Is the subject best understood as an object, process, event, relation, model, method, project, protocol, dataset, person, organization, or something else?
* Which distinctions are fundamental and which are conveniences of presentation?
* What relationships connect this object to others?
* What boundary defines the object, and what changes when that boundary moves?

The project has often leaned toward a '''process-relational''' view: entities can be understood through change, interaction, context, and relation rather than only as fixed substances. V1 applied this to projects, ideas, digital objects, and knowledge itself.

This orientation is useful, but V2 does not constitutionalize one metaphysical school as the only ontology available to every domain.

== Epistemology — knowing, evidence, justification ==

Epistemology asks '''how we know'''. It includes:

* sources and evidence;
* observation and measurement;
* proof and deduction;
* inference and modeling;
* interpretation and perspective;
* uncertainty and confidence;
* contradiction and falsification;
* provenance and revision history;
* limits of observers, instruments, models, and agents.

The historical system emphasized radical skepticism and radical epistemic humility: no source, institution, agent, or constitutional document is beyond examination.

A mature epistemic layer should preserve not merely a status label but a '''confidence trail'''—the inspectable route by which a claim acquired its present standing.

== Axiology — value, purpose, attention ==

Axiology asks '''what matters'''. It includes ethics, but it is broader than ethics.

Every knowledge system allocates attention. Every decision to preserve, feature, relate, suppress, verify, revisit, or ignore material carries a value judgment whether or not that judgment is acknowledged.

In practical terms, axiology asks:

* Why is this worth preserving?
* What outcome are we optimizing for?
* What risks or harms require protection?
* What deserves scarce attention?
* What trade-offs are acceptable?
* What must remain invariant even when implementation changes?

V1 made this point sharply: without explicit value, a knowledge system risks becoming an undifferentiated heap in which everything is equally important and therefore nothing is.

== Recursive relationship ==

The three axes constrain and reshape one another.

=== Ontology ↔ Epistemology ===

What we believe exists affects what evidence we seek; the evidence available can force us to revise what we believe exists.

A dataset presupposes an ontology of measurable entities and attributes. A theory of consciousness presupposes claims about minds, observers, experience, or computation. A historical claim depends on what counts as an event, document, institution, actor, or cause.

=== Epistemology ↔ Axiology ===

Values influence what questions are investigated, which errors are considered costly, how conservative a threshold should be, and which evidence deserves attention. Epistemic integrity in turn constrains value-driven narratives by requiring that desire not masquerade as evidence.

=== Axiology ↔ Ontology ===

What a system recognizes as real affects what it can value. What it values affects which distinctions it makes first-class.

Privacy, for example, requires an ontology that can distinguish public from protected information. Human agency becomes actionable only when humans, agents, decisions, and responsibility are represented distinctly enough to reason about them.

== Semantics — the bridge of meaning ==

One major V1 formulation named the triad '''Axiology, Semantics, and Ontology'''. It treated Semantics as the bridge between value and being: values must be expressed, ontological claims must be communicated, and machine-readable relationships give concepts contextual meaning.

That insight is preserved.

V2 does not discard Semantics; it changes its architectural role. Semantics is treated as a '''bridging discipline and implementation surface''' across the root axes rather than replacing Epistemology as a root question.

This matters because meaning and justification are not the same thing. A semantic triple can represent a claim precisely without establishing that the claim is well-supported.

== Logic, language, and interpretation ==

The larger architecture includes other cross-cutting domains:

* '''Logic''' — inferential coherence and formal relation.
* '''Semantics''' — meaning and representation.
* '''Language''' — expression, naming, and communication.
* '''Phenomenology''' — structures of experience.
* '''Hermeneutics''' — interpretation of texts, symbols, and contexts.
* '''Praxeology / action theory''' — action, choice, and consequence.

These can bridge multiple axes. They should not be forced into a root taxonomy merely to make the diagram symmetrical.

== Earlier V1 triads ==

The V1 corpus contains multiple genuine formulations. They are part of the intellectual history, not errors to erase.

=== Axiology / Semantics / Ontology ===

The long V1 [[BITwiki Foundational Triad]] centered on value, meaning, and being and used the circumpunct as a symbol of their interweaving. It developed important ideas around attention allocation, relational meaning, process ontology, digital objects, and the recursive relation between value, representation, and being.

=== Values / Knowledge / Structure ===

The same V1 page also described the foundation operationally as:

# Values — what we care about and why;
# Knowledge — how we know what we claim;
# Structure — what exists in our domain.

This is very close to the current Axiology / Epistemology / Ontology formulation.

=== Recursion / Radical Epistemic Humility / Ontological Transparency ===

A second embedded V1 formulation treated three operating principles as the "Foundational Triad": recursion, radical epistemic humility, and ontological transparency.

V2 preserves these as '''system principles''' rather than metaphysical root axes:

* Recursion describes how the system can inspect and improve itself.
* Radical epistemic humility constrains how confidently it claims to know.
* Ontological transparency requires that classifications and assumptions remain inspectable.

They remain foundational in importance even though they now occupy a different layer of the architecture.

== The circumpunct ◯ ==

V1 used the circumpunct—a central point enclosed by a circle—as a diagram of the knowledge field: center, boundary, interior, and encounter with other systems.

The symbol remains useful as historical and conceptual language. V2 does not require it as literal metaphysics.

== Application in BITwiki ==

For a knowledge page:

{| class="wikitable"
! Axis !! Practical questions
|-
| Ontology || What is this? What type of thing is it? What boundaries and relationships define it?
|-
| Epistemology || How is this known? What evidence, method, uncertainty, contradiction, or provenance bears on it?
|-
| Axiology || Why does it matter? What values, risks, priorities, or consequences make it worth preserving or acting upon?
|}

Not every page needs three explicit sections. The triad is a reasoning lens, not a rigid page template.

== Application in BITCORE ==

At the system level:

* Ontology informs internal structure, identity space, entity types, relationships, and boundaries.
* Epistemology informs validation, evidence handling, confidence, source evaluation, and truth acquisition.
* Axiology informs optimization, prioritization, purpose, safety boundaries, and what should become durable knowledge.

== Governing principle ==

'''The map is not the territory, but the map must be clean in order to be able to traverse it.'''

The triad is therefore not presented as the final metaphysics of reality. It is a clean operational map for asking three kinds of questions that the system must not confuse.

